Syntax: weave <item>

Weave picks a worn thing apart and winds what it was made of back into a
skein of thread.  It works on anything of a soft material -- cloth, wool,
fur, leather, hemp, silk -- whatever kind of thing it is, including the
clippings a tailor leaves behind.

It is the crucible's twin, and it works the same way.  Half the weight comes
back as thread.  Every pulse is counted, landed or missed, and the ratio
between them grades the skein, which decides how much of what the piece
carried survives being sewn into something new.

Fine fibre resists: common cloth comes apart easily, silk and laminate fight
you the whole way, and nothing buys that back the way advanced blacksmithing
buys back the crucible.

See also: SEW, SKEIN, TAILOR, SMELT
